// The boundary is a cluster box, and the serialize/deserialize hooks are edge
// labels rather than nodes. As nodes there were four of them around one
// execute(), so the picture read as a six-step pipeline whose middle step
// happened to be the reader's code. Each hook belongs to a crossing, and a
// crossing is an edge, so labelling the edges puts the hooks where they act and
// leaves the boundary as the only thing drawn.
//
// BOTH SIDES ARE BOXES NOW (review: "can add after result boxes clarifying that
// this is the main thread"). One box and three loose nodes read as "the worker,
// and some other stuff"; two boxes read as a boundary, which is the subject. It
// also pays for itself twice over in label text: `statusCallback` used to have
// to SAY "on the main thread", and the green fill used to be the only thing
// distinguishing the caller's two nodes from the worker's.
//
// Colour is now redundant with the boxes and is kept anyway — it is what carries
// the distinction into the thumbnail, where the cluster labels are unreadable.
//
// EVERY LABEL IS A NAME (review: "this looks a bit chaotic. wordiness needs to
// be substantially reduced. the serializeArguments has too much detail for
// example"). Each node and edge carries what the thing is CALLED and stops
// there, so the figure answers "what runs, where, in what order" and the page
// answers everything else. What came off it is all on the page under the
// figure: which hooks are yours to override, what the serialize step does to
// refNames and functions, where execute() should read its arguments from, and
// the transferList form of a return.
//
// The review offered a simplified figure beside an extended one as a fallback,
// and one short figure turned out to be enough — a second diagram of the same
// five nodes would need the reader to work out what changed between them.
//
// Three layout constraints, each of which cost a re-render to find:
//
// The main thread's nodes must be CONTIGUOUS IN RANK for dot to give them a box
// that does not swallow the worker's. `call` at rank 0 and `result` at rank 3
// (the shape of the round trip, TB) cannot be boxed at all: the cluster would
// span the worker's ranks and dot draws the two rectangles overlapping. So it is
// one rank per side — `rankdir=LR`, main thread rank 0, worker rank 1, each side
// a column of its own — and the return trip is a back edge between adjacent
// columns, `constraint=false` so it adds no rank.
//
// No `{ rank=same; exec; status }`. Ranking an outside node against one inside a
// cluster EVICTS it ("exec was already in a rankset, deleted from cluster"), so
// the boundary box loses the node it exists to enclose. `status` is inside
// cluster_main now, ordered against its siblings by the invisible edges below,
// which is a rankset entirely within one cluster and so does not evict anything.
//
// Each cluster's label is short and left-justified because an incoming arrow
// enters the box at its edge midpoint, which a centred label gets struck
// through by. Sticky-per-sessionId is the paragraph under the figure rather than
// a second line here.
digraph rpc_lifecycle {
  rankdir=LR
  labelloc=t
  fontname="Helvetica"
  fontsize=18
  label="One RPC call across the worker boundary"
  nodesep=0.45
  // 1.1 was sized for four-line crossing labels. At one line each, that much
  // gap leaves the two columns floating; the labels still fit at 0.8.
  ranksep=0.8

  node [shape=box, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=12, style=filled]
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, color="#555555"]

  subgraph cluster_main {
    label="main thread"
    labelloc=t
    labeljust=l
    fontname="Helvetica"
    fontsize=12
    fontcolor="#33691e"
    style="filled,rounded"
    color="#aed581"
    fillcolor="#f4faec"

    call [label="rpcManager.call(sessionId, name, args)", fillcolor="#dcedc8"]
    status [
      label="statusCallback"
      style="filled,dashed"
      fillcolor="#fff3e0"
      color="#ef6c00"
    ]
    result [label="result", peripheries=2, fillcolor="#dcedc8"]

    // order down the column: the call, then what it is reported to while it
    // runs, then what comes back. Invisible and inside the cluster, so no node
    // is evicted from a box by a rankset.
    //
    // Written bottom-up because that is what dot does with it: within a rankset
    // the edge direction sets the order along the rank, and under rankdir=LR a
    // rank runs UP the page, so `call -> status -> result` puts the call at the
    // bottom. Rendered both ways to be sure.
    { rank=same; result -> status -> call [style=invis] }
  }

  subgraph cluster_worker {
    label="web worker"
    labelloc=t
    labeljust=l
    fontname="Helvetica"
    fontsize=12
    fontcolor="#0d47a1"
    style="filled,rounded"
    color="#90caf9"
    fillcolor="#eaf4fd"

    exec [label="execute()  ← your code", fillcolor="#e1f5fe"]
    ret [label="return value", fillcolor="#e3f2fd"]
    // ONE COLUMN, like the main thread's. Left to its own rank `ret` lands a
    // column further right, which puts the return crossing at the far side of
    // the drawing from the node it returns to — dot drew it as a sweep under
    // the entire figure with its label stranded in the middle. Same rank, so
    // both crossings are between adjacent columns and the picture is the
    // sequence diagram it wants to be.
    { rank=same; ret -> exec [dir=back] }
  }

  // The two hooks ride the crossings they belong to.
  call -> exec [label="  serializeArguments()  "]
  ret -> result [label="  deserializeReturn()  ", constraint=false]

  // The one function-shaped thing that crosses, and it never actually crosses:
  // the worker holds a side channel, the function itself is in the box it
  // started in.
  exec -> status [
    label="  progress  "
    style=dashed
    constraint=false
    color="#ef6c00"
    fontcolor="#ef6c00"
  ]
}
